### Scenario 1: Pre-filling a form with existing data (e.g., Editing a Profile)
If you want to auto-populate fields when a component loads (like a user editing their profile), you'll use React's `useEffect` hook to fetch the data from your Rails API and `useState` to populate the form.

**Ruby on Rails (Controller):**
```ruby
# config/routes.rb
namespace :api do
  resources :users, only: [:show]
end

# app/controllers/api/users_controller.rb
class Api::UsersController < ApplicationController
  def show
    user = User.find(params[:id])
    render json: user.as_json(only: [:id, :name, :email, :phone])
  end
end
```

**React (Frontend):**
```javascript
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

const EditUserForm = ({ userId }) => {
  const [formData, setFormData] = useState({
    name: '',
    email: '',
    phone: ''
  });
  const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState(true);

  useEffect(() => {
    // Fetch data from Rails API
    fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`)
      .then((response) => response.json())
      .then((data) => {
        // Auto-populate the form fields with the fetched data
        setFormData({
          name: data.name || '',
          email: data.email || '',
          phone: data.phone || ''
        });
        setIsLoading(false);
      })
      .catch((error) => {
        console.error("Error fetching user data:", error);
        setIsLoading(false);
      });
  }, [userId]);

  const handleChange = (e) => {
    setFormData({ ...formData, [e.target.name]: e.target.value });
  };

  if (isLoading) return <p>Loading data...</p>;

  return (
    <form>
      <label>Name:</label>
      <input type="text" name="name" value={formData.name} onChange={handleChange} />

      <label>Email:</label>
      <input type="email" name="email" value={formData.email} onChange={handleChange} />

      <label>Phone:</label>
      <input type="text" name="phone" value={formData.phone} onChange={handleChange} />
    </form>
  );
};

export default EditUserForm;
```

